From 44613bba59d4ee05f5d9b4471f0b76d2710c5121 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:51:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(front): read a LET's INSTANCE in a temporal formula, and
 end the subscript reading's every recursion

The definitions an INSTANCE in a LET makes are the LET's own, which the
copies' bodies name by keys no global definition has. Reading the
specification, the front end followed such a name only where it was
written as local, so `Spec == LET I == INSTANCE Inner WITH a <- x IN
I!ISpec` read as a specification without an initial predicate. It now
looks a name no global definition has up in the LET, which reads the
specification as TLC does. The decline of every LET with an INSTANCE
in a temporal formula is gone, so a property such as EWD687a's
TreeWithRoot runs as on main again; only a SPECIFICATION reaching a
parameterized INSTANCE in a LET still declines, as the front end
evaluates its definitions as of constant level.

The subscript collector bounded its own reading of [][Next]_v, but not
the test of whether a definition TLC computes as it starts holds a
value, which followed `RECURSIVE F`, `F == F` without end and
overflowed the process's stack, nor the trial reading it makes of such
a definition, which took a reading that doesn't end for one it can't
follow. Both now share the reading's bound and decline as it does,
whatever -nowarning says, as TLC's collector overflows its stack there.
